1
00:00:00,878 --> 00:00:04,119
part 2 chapter 8.

2
00:00:04,119 --> 00:00:07,000
They had done it, they had done it at last!

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,840
The room that we're standing in was long shaped and softly lit.

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:14,860
The telescreen was dimmed to a low murmur; the richness of the dark blue

5
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:18,218
carpet gave one the impression of trading on velvet.

6
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:21,759
At the far end of the room O'Brien was sitting at a table under a green shaded

7
00:00:21,759 --> 00:00:24,878
lamp, with a massive papers on either side of him.

8
00:00:24,878 --> 00:00:29,919
He had not bothered to look up when the servant showed Julia and Winston in.

9
00:00:29,919 --> 00:00:34,718
Winston's heart was thumping so hard that he doubted whether he would be able to speak.

10
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:38,619
They had done it, they had done it at last, was all he could think.

11
00:00:38,619 --> 00:00:43,060
It had been a rash act to come here at all, and sheer folly to arrive together;

12
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:47,979
though it was true that they had come by different routes and only met on O'Brien's doorstep.

13
00:00:47,979 --> 00:00:51,878
But merely to walk into such a place needed an effort of the nerve.

14
00:00:51,878 --> 00:00:55,599
It was only on very rare occasions that one saw inside the Dwelling Places of

15
00:00:55,599 --> 00:01:00,340
the Inner Party, or even penetrated into the quarter of the town where they lived.

16
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:04,180
The whole atmosphere of the huge block of flats, the richness and spaciousness

17
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:08,797
of everything, the unfamiliar smells of good food and good tobacco, the silent

18
00:01:08,797 --> 00:01:12,700
and incredibly rapid lifts sliding up and down, the white jacketed servants

19
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,779
hurrying to and fro— —everything was intimidating.

20
00:01:16,779 --> 00:01:20,500
Although he had a good pretext for coming here, he was haunted at every step

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,740
by the fear that a black uniformed God would suddenly appear from around the

22
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:27,218
corner, demand his papers and order him to get out.

23
00:01:27,218 --> 00:01:31,419
O'Brien's servant, however, had admitted the two of them without demur.

24
00:01:31,419 --> 00:01:35,319
He was a small, dark-haired man in a white jacket, with a diamond shaped

25
00:01:35,319 --> 00:01:39,400
completely expressionless face which might have been that of a Chinese.

26
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,360
The passage down which he led them was softly carpeted, with cream papered walls

27
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,599
and white wainscoting, all exquisitely clean.

28
00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:50,560
That too was intimidating. Winston could not remember ever to have seen a

29
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,637
passageway whose walls were not grimy from the contact of human bodies.

30
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:59,439
O'Brien had a slip of paper between his fingers and seemed to be studying it intently.

31
00:01:59,439 --> 00:02:03,218
His heavy face, bent down so that one could see the line of the nose, looked

32
00:02:03,218 --> 00:02:05,740
both formidable and intelligent.

33
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:08,797
For perhaps 20 seconds he sat without stirring.

34
00:02:08,797 --> 00:02:11,797
Then he pulled the speak right towards him and wrapped out a message in the

35
00:02:11,797 --> 00:02:16,540
hybrid jargon of the Ministries: Items one comma 5 comma 7 approved

36
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,520
full-wise stop suggestion contained item 6 double plus ridiculous virgin crime

37
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:26,740
think canceled stop and proceed construction-wise anti-getting plus full estimates

38
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:30,218
machinery overhead stop end message.

39
00:02:30,218 --> 00:02:35,080
He rose deliberately from his chair and came towards them across the soundless carpet.

40
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:38,259
A little of the official atmosphere seemed to have fallen away from him with

41
00:02:38,259 --> 00:02:42,520
the new speak words, but his expression was grimmer than usual, as though he were

42
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,159
not pleased at being disturbed.

43
00:02:45,159 --> 00:02:48,637
The terror that Winston already felt was suddenly shot through by a streak of

44
00:02:48,637 --> 00:02:50,378
ordinary embarrassment.

45
00:02:50,378 --> 00:02:54,457
It seems to him quite possible that he had simply made a stupid mistake. For

46
00:02:54,457 --> 00:02:57,520
what evidence had he in reality that O'Brien was any kind of political

47
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:01,419
conspirator? Nothing but a flash of the eyes and a single equivocal remark:

48
00:03:01,419 --> 00:03:05,560
beyond that, only his own secret imaginings, founded on a dream.

49
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,560
He could not even fall back on the pretense that he had come to borrow the

50
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:12,457
Dictionary, because in that case Julia's presence was impossible to explain.

51
00:03:12,457 --> 00:03:16,240
As O'Brien passed the tele screener thoughts seemed to strike him.

52
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:20,919
He stopped, turned aside and pressed a switch on the wall. There was a sharp snap.

53
00:03:20,919 --> 00:03:23,378
The voice had stopped.

54
00:03:23,378 --> 00:03:26,797
Julia uttered a tiny sound, a sort of squeak of surprise.

55
00:03:26,797 --> 00:03:30,520
Even in the midst of Hispanic Winston was too much taken aback to be able to

56
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:32,740
hold his tongue.

57
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:35,740
'You can turn it off!' he said.

58
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:39,819
'Yes,' said O'Brien, 'we can turn it off. We have that privilege.'

59
00:03:39,819 --> 00:03:44,680
He was opposite them now. His solid form towered over the pair of them, and the

60
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:47,378
expression on his face was still indecipherable.

61
00:03:47,378 --> 00:03:51,580
He was waiting, somewhat sternly, for Winston to speak, but about what?

62
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:54,878
Even now it was quite conceivable that he was simply a busy man wondering

63
00:03:54,878 --> 00:03:57,939
irritably why he had been interrupted.

64
00:03:57,939 --> 00:03:59,619
Nobody spoke.

65
00:03:59,619 --> 00:04:03,457
After the stopping of the telescreen the room seemed deadly silent.

66
00:04:03,457 --> 00:04:06,457
The seconds marched past, enormous.

67
00:04:06,457 --> 00:04:11,020
With difficulty Winston continued to keep his eyes fixed on her brands

68
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:15,457
and suddenly the grim face broke down into what might have been the beginnings of a smile.

69
00:04:15,457 --> 00:04:20,500
With his characteristic gesture a brand resettled his spectacles on his nose.

70
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:23,439
'Shall I say it, or will you?' he said.

71
00:04:23,439 --> 00:04:27,700
'I will say it,' said Winston promptly the thing is really turned off?'

72
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:31,419
'Yes, everything is turned off. We are alone.'

73
00:04:31,419 --> 00:04:35,439
'We have come here because— He paused realizing for the first time

74
00:04:35,439 --> 00:04:39,279
the vagueness of his own motives. Since he did not in fact know what kind of

75
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:43,599
help he expected from her Brian it was not easy to say why he had come here.

76
00:04:43,599 --> 00:04:49,560
He went on, conscious that what he was saying must sound both feeble and pretentious:

77
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,840
'We believe that there is some kind of conspiracy, some kind of secret

78
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:57,099
organization working against the Party, and that you were involved in it.

79
00:04:57,099 --> 00:05:01,479
We want to join it and work for it. We are enemies of the Party.

80
00:05:01,479 --> 00:05:07,659
We just believe in the principles of insock We are thought criminals We are also adulterers.

81
00:05:07,659 --> 00:05:11,500
I tell you this because we want to put ourselves at your mercy.

82
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,200
If you want us to incriminate ourselves in any other way, we are ready.'

83
00:05:16,419 --> 00:05:20,259
He stopped and glanced over his shoulder, with the feeling that the door had opened.

84
00:05:20,259 --> 00:05:24,279
Sure enough, the little yellow-faced servant had come in without knocking.

85
00:05:24,279 --> 00:05:28,297
Winston saw that he was carrying a tree with a decanter and glasses.

86
00:05:28,297 --> 00:05:32,740
'Martin is one of us,' said O'Brien impassively. 'Bring the drinks over here,

87
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:34,957
Martin. Put them on the round table.

88
00:05:34,957 --> 00:05:39,099
Have we enough chairs? Then we may as well sit down and talk in comfort.

89
00:05:39,099 --> 00:05:42,878
Bring a chair for yourself, Martin. This is business. You can stop being a servant

90
00:05:42,878 --> 00:05:45,279
for the next 10 minutes.'

91
00:05:45,279 --> 00:05:48,700
The little man sat down quiet at his ease, and yet still with a servant like

92
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:52,360
air, the heir of a valley enjoying a privilege.

93
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:55,180
Winston regarded him out of the corner of his eye.

94
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:59,200
It struck him that the man's whole life was playing a part, and that he felt it

95
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:03,159
to be dangerous to drop his assumed personality even for a moment

96
00:06:03,159 --> 00:06:06,700
LeBron took the decanter by the neck and filled up the glasses with a dark red

97
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:12,580
liquid. It aroused in Winston dim memories of something seen long ago on a wall or a hoarding—

98
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:16,119
—a vast bottle composed of electric lights which seemed to move up and down

99
00:06:16,119 --> 00:06:18,939
and pour its contents into a glass.

100
00:06:18,939 --> 00:06:22,779
Seen from the top the stuff looked almost black, but in the Decatur it

101
00:06:22,779 --> 00:06:26,259
gleamed like a ruby. It had a sour sweet smell.

102
00:06:26,259 --> 00:06:31,479
He saw Julia pick up her glass and sniff at it with frank curiosity.

103
00:06:31,479 --> 00:06:35,378
'It is called wine,' said O'Brien with a faint smile. 'You will have read about it

104
00:06:35,378 --> 00:06:39,700
in books, no doubt. Not much of it gets to the Outer Party I'm afraid.'

105
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:45,218
His face grew solemn again, and he raised his glass: 'I think it is fitting that we should

106
00:06:45,218 --> 00:06:47,020
begin by drinking a health.

107
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:51,159
To our Leader: To Emmanuel Goldstein.'

108
00:06:51,159 --> 00:06:54,099
Winston took up his glass with a certain eagerness.

109
00:06:54,099 --> 00:06:56,797
Wine was a thing he had read and dreamed about.

110
00:06:56,797 --> 00:07:00,637
Like the glass paperweight or Mr cherrington's half-remembered rhymes, it

111
00:07:00,637 --> 00:07:04,900
belonged to the vanished, romantic past, the olden time as he likes to call it in

112
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:06,580
his secret thoughts.

113
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:10,360
For some reason he had always thought of wine as having an intensely sweet taste,

114
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:14,619
like that of blackberry jam, and an immediate intoxicating effect.

115
00:07:14,619 --> 00:07:19,000
Actually, when he came to swallow it, the stuff was distinctly disappointing. The

116
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,479
truth was that after years of gin drinking he could barely taste it.

117
00:07:22,479 --> 00:07:25,240
He sat down the empty glass.

118
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:27,340
'Then there is such a person as Goldstein?'

119
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:28,419
he said.

120
00:07:28,419 --> 00:07:33,279
'Yes, there is such a person, and he is alive. Where, I do not know.'

121
00:07:33,279 --> 00:07:38,560
'And the conspiracy— —the organization is It real is it not simply an invention

122
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:40,718
of the Thought Police?'

123
00:07:40,718 --> 00:07:43,240
'No, it is real. The Brotherhood, we call it.

124
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:46,659
You will never learn much more about the Brotherhood than that it exists and that

125
00:07:46,659 --> 00:07:48,159
you belong to it.

126
00:07:48,159 --> 00:07:50,797
I will come back to that presently.'

127
00:07:50,797 --> 00:07:54,819
He looked at his wrist watch It is unwise even for members of the

128
00:07:54,819 --> 00:07:58,060
Inner Party to turn off the telescreen for more than half an hour.

129
00:07:58,060 --> 00:08:01,840
You ought not to have come here together, and you will have to leave separately.

130
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,680
You, Comrade'— '—he bowed his head to Julia—' —'will leave first.

131
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:07,779
We have about 20 minutes at our disposal.

132
00:08:07,779 --> 00:08:11,860
You will understand that I must start by asking you certain questions.

133
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:15,400
In general terms, what are you prepared to do?'

134
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:20,740
'Anything that we are capable of,' said Winston her brand had turned himself a little in

135
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:22,779
his chair so that he was facing Winston.

136
00:08:22,779 --> 00:08:26,378
He almost ignored Julia, seeming to take it for granted that Winston could speak

137
00:08:26,378 --> 00:08:30,340
for her. For a moment the lids flitted down over his eyes.

138
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:34,779
He began asking his questions in a low, expressionless voice, as though this were

139
00:08:34,779 --> 00:08:40,119
a routine, a sort of catechism, most of whose answers were known to him already.

140
00:08:40,119 --> 00:08:42,520
'You were prepared to give your lives?' 'Yes.'

141
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:45,159
'You were prepared to commit murder?'

142
00:08:45,159 --> 00:08:50,500
'Yes.' 'To commit acts of sabotage which may cause the death of hundreds of innocent people?'

143
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:51,878
'Yes.'

144
00:08:51,878 --> 00:08:54,400
'To betray your country to foreign powers?'

145
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:55,900
'Yes.'

146
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:59,619
'You were prepared to cheat, to forge, to blackmail, to corrupt the minds of

147
00:08:59,619 --> 00:09:03,520
children, to distribute habit-forming drugs, to encourage prostitution, to

148
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:06,759
disseminate venereal diseases— —to do anything which is likely to cause

149
00:09:06,759 --> 00:09:09,878
demoralization and weaken the power of the Party?'

150
00:09:09,878 --> 00:09:11,500
'Yes.'

151
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:15,159
'If, for example, it would somehow serve our interests to throw sulfuric acid in

152
00:09:15,159 --> 00:09:17,680
a child's face— —are you prepared to do that?'

153
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:19,240
'Yes.'

154
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:22,360
'You are prepared to lose your identity and live out the rest of your life as a

155
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:25,297
waiter or a dock worker Yes.'

156
00:09:25,297 --> 00:09:29,080
'You are prepared to commit suicide, if and when we order you to do so?'

157
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:30,400
'Yes.'

158
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:34,779
'You are prepared, the two of you, to separate and never see one another again?'

159
00:09:34,779 --> 00:09:37,297
'No broken Julia.

160
00:09:37,297 --> 00:09:41,560
It appeared to Winston that a long time passed before he answered. For a moment

161
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:45,218
he seemed even to have been deprived of the power of speech.

162
00:09:45,218 --> 00:09:48,819
His tongue worked soundlessly, forming the opening syllables first of one word,

163
00:09:48,819 --> 00:09:52,060
then of the other, over and over again.

164
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:55,957
Until he had said it, he did not know which word he was going to say.

165
00:09:55,957 --> 00:09:58,599
'No,' he said finally.

166
00:09:58,599 --> 00:10:04,180
'You did well to tell me,' said O'Brien. 'It is necessary for us to know everything.'

167
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:09,580
He turned himself toward Julia and added in a voice with somewhat more expression in it:

168
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:12,878
'Do you understand that even if he survives, it maybe as a different person?

169
00:10:12,878 --> 00:10:16,297
We may be obliged to give him a new identity.

170
00:10:16,297 --> 00:10:20,319
His face, his movements, the shape of his hands, the color of his hair— —even his

171
00:10:20,319 --> 00:10:21,759
voice would be different.

172
00:10:21,759 --> 00:10:24,457
And you yourself might have become a different person.

173
00:10:24,457 --> 00:10:29,080
Our surgeons can alter people beyond recognition. Sometimes it is necessary.

174
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:32,619
Sometimes we even amputate a limb.'

175
00:10:32,619 --> 00:10:37,718
Winston could not help snatching another side long glance at Martin's Mongolian face.

176
00:10:37,718 --> 00:10:39,939
There were no scars that he could see.

177
00:10:39,939 --> 00:10:43,779
Julia had turned a shade paler, so that her freckles were showing, but she faced

178
00:10:43,779 --> 00:10:45,819
her brand boldly.

179
00:10:45,819 --> 00:10:52,000
She murmured something that seemed to be a scent Good and that is settled.'

180
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,957
There was a silver box of cigarettes on the table. With a rather absent bounded

181
00:10:55,957 --> 00:10:59,797
air O'Brien pushed them towards the others, took one himself, then stood up

182
00:10:59,797 --> 00:11:04,659
and began to pace slowly to and fro, as though he could think better standing.

183
00:11:04,659 --> 00:11:08,259
They were very good cigarettes, very thick and well packed with an unfamiliar

184
00:11:08,259 --> 00:11:10,119
silkiness in the paper.

185
00:11:10,119 --> 00:11:12,580
O'Brien looked at his wristwatch again.

186
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:16,479
'You had better go back to your pantry, Martin,' he said. 'I shall switch on in a

187
00:11:16,479 --> 00:11:20,560
quarter of an hour. Take a good look at these comrades' faces before you go. You

188
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:24,400
will be seeing them again. I may not.'

189
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:27,457
Exactly as they had done at the front door, the little man's dark eyes

190
00:11:27,457 --> 00:11:29,560
flickered over their faces.

191
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:32,378
There was not a trace of fringiness in his manner.

192
00:11:32,378 --> 00:11:37,419
He was memorizing their appearance, but he felt no interest in them, or appeared to feel none.

193
00:11:37,419 --> 00:11:42,939
It occurred to Winston that a synthetic face was perhaps incapable of changing its expression.

194
00:11:42,939 --> 00:11:47,200
Without speaking or giving any kind of salutation, Martin went out, closing the

195
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:49,360
door silently behind him.

196
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:52,957
O'Brien was strolling up and down, one hand in the pocket of his black overalls,

197
00:11:52,957 --> 00:11:55,659
the other holding his cigarette.

198
00:11:55,659 --> 00:11:59,797
'You understand,' he said, 'that you will be fighting in the dark. You will always be

199
00:11:59,797 --> 00:12:00,939
in the dark.

200
00:12:00,939 --> 00:12:04,957
You will receive orders and you will obey them, without knowing why.

201
00:12:04,957 --> 00:12:08,137
Later I shall send you a book from which you will learn the true nature of the

202
00:12:08,137 --> 00:12:12,457
society we live in, and the strategy by which we shall destroy it.

203
00:12:12,457 --> 00:12:15,759
When you have read the book, you will be full members of the Brotherhood.

204
00:12:15,759 --> 00:12:19,840
But between the general aims that we are fighting for, and the immediate tasks of

205
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,297
the moment, you will never know anything.

206
00:12:22,297 --> 00:12:25,659
I tell you that the Brotherhood exists, but I cannot tell you whether it numbers

207
00:12:25,659 --> 00:12:27,819
100 members, or 10 million.

208
00:12:27,819 --> 00:12:30,819
From your personal knowledge you will never be able to say that it numbers

209
00:12:30,819 --> 00:12:34,779
even as many as it doesn't You will have three or four contacts, who

210
00:12:34,779 --> 00:12:37,840
will be renewed from time to time as they disappear.

211
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:40,900
As this was your first contact, it will be preserved.

212
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:43,718
When you receive orders, they will come from me.

213
00:12:43,718 --> 00:12:47,797
If we find it necessary to communicate with you, it will be through Martin.

214
00:12:47,797 --> 00:12:52,060
When you are finally caught, you will confess. That is unavoidable. But you will

215
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:55,060
have very little to confess, other than your own actions.

216
00:12:55,060 --> 00:12:58,900
You will not be able to betray more than a handful of unimportant people.

217
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:01,540
Probably you will not even betray me.

218
00:13:01,540 --> 00:13:04,957
By that time I may be dead, or I shall have become a different person, with a

219
00:13:04,957 --> 00:13:06,457
different face.'

220
00:13:06,457 --> 00:13:11,378
He continued to move to and fro over the soft carpet. In spite of the bulkiness of

221
00:13:11,378 --> 00:13:15,637
his body there was a remarkable grace in his movements. It came out even in the

222
00:13:15,637 --> 00:13:19,718
gesture with which he thrust a hand into his pocket, or manipulated a cigarette.

223
00:13:19,718 --> 00:13:23,860
More even than of strength, he gave an impression of confidence and of an

224
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:26,500
understanding tinted by irony.

225
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:30,159
However much he known as he might be, he had nothing of the single-mindedness

226
00:13:30,159 --> 00:13:32,500
that belongs to a fanatic.

227
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:36,700
